  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 239 PM EDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nCurrent temperatures have warmed to the mid-70s along the lakeshore \nand low-80s inland this afternoon. Skies are expected to remain \nmostly clear tonight as drier air moves in behind the occluded front \nthat passed eastward through the U.P. this morning. Lows will fall \nto the upper 50s near the lakeshore and the lower 50s to upper 40s \ninland, with generally calm winds across the CWA overnight. Tomorrow \nis expected to be pleasant, with highs in the mid-70s near the \nlakeshore and upper 70s to low 80s inland, light winds, and partly \ncloudy skies.\n\nThe next chance for precipitation, limited mainly to the \nsouthwestern U.P. is expected to occur Thursday evening into Friday \nmorning as a shortwave trough passes across northern Wisconsin. At \nthe present, the best chances for precipitation (around 40%) are \nexpected to be near the WI/MI state line early Friday morning. \nHowever, there is still considerable uncertainty in the CAMs as to \nthe exact timing and extent of precipitation for our area. \nSignificant rainfall is not expected with these showers, as the \nprobability of 0.25 inches of rain or more is only between 20-40%.\n\nAs the closed low over Ontario progresses eastward, it will be \nreplaced by significant upper-level ridging over the central CONUS, \nresulting in northwest flow across the Great Lakes for the weekend. \nHigh temperatures are expected to remain above-normal in the mid to \nupper 70s at least through Sunday due to the influence of this \nridging pattern. Early next week brings the next best chance for \nprecipitation, with current model guidance indicating a 40-60% \nchance of at least 0.25 inches of rain by 8am Wednesday, however, \nuncertainty is still high at this time for the timing and extent of \nprecipitation. Breezy conditions are anticipated as this system \nmoves through. NBM probabilities of wind gusts at or above 40mph are \nbetween 30-60% in the Keweenaw, highest on Wednesday in the wake of \nthe cold front.",
